4. Problem Statement: HRS Procurement Intelligence Agents  
Challenge: "Build an AI-Powered Procurement Intelligence Platform for Corporate Hotel Negotiation & Supplier Submission" 
 
Design and implement a web-based platform where specialized AI agents accelerate and automate the two highest-friction points in the HRS corporate procurement cycle:  
(1) preparing consultants for local hotel negotiations after rebid flagging, and (2) supporting hotels through the bid submission process inside the supplier application. Think "AI co-pilot for both sides of the negotiation table" — one agent fleet arms HRS consultants with data-driven leverage, while another agent fleet ensures hotels submit compliant bids without needing human support. The agents retrieve data from multiple systems in parallel, reason over RFP context, and act autonomously within defined guardrails. 
Tier 1: Must Have 
Negotiation Brief Agent — when Copilot flags a hotel for local negotiation, the agent auto-assembles a pre-call brief containing: bid vs. target gap (absolute + %), client room nights delivered last year + YoY trend, NDS & buyer/seller market classification, comparable hotel acceptances in same destination, stickiness score, HPO walk-away alternative 
Agent Creator (name, model, system prompt, data domain assignment, memory config) 
Model Selector (GPT, Claude, Gemini, DeepSeek) 
Prompt Editor with Versioning — configure and iterate on agent system prompts 
Agent Chat — query any agent conversationally: "What leverage do we have with Hotel X in Munich?" 
Agent Registry (list/grid with status: active, syncing, idle, error) 
 
Tier 2: Should Have 
Supplier Question Resolution Agent — embedded in supplier app; answers hotel questions ("What is LRA?", "Why is my rate field blocked?") by retrieving from RFP briefing doc, GBTA requirements, and hotel-level eRFP instructions simultaneously 
Pre-Submission Compliance Agent — scans completed bids against hotel-level instructions before submit: validates rate type, breakfast structure, mandatory GBTA fields; surfaces specific errors with fix instructions 
Deadline Urgency Agent — monitors submission progress per hotel against 2-week window; proactively reaches out at day 7 and day 12 with targeted status (incomplete fields, outstanding issues, direct link back) 
MCP tool/resource assignment per agent (e.g., hpo_lookup, pe_data, datahub_query, omc_master, erfp_config, briefing_doc_retriever) 
Talking Points Generator — produces 3–4 data-grounded negotiation talking points per hotel (not a script, but key facts to lead with) 
Post-Call Outcome Logger — consultant logs result in 3 clicks; agent updates negotiation queue, flags counter-offers within acceptable range, marks destination resolved/pending 
Consultant Dashboard — prioritized negotiation queue ranked by leverage score + savings potential 
 
 
Tier 3: Wow Factor 
A2A Multi-Agent Pipeline: Negotiation Brief Agent retrieves data → Talking Points Agent generates leverage arguments → Post-Call Agent logs outcome → Queue Manager Agent re-prioritizes remaining hotels 
Supplier-Side A2A Pipeline: Question Agent resolves query → Compliance Agent validates → Urgency Agent monitors → Operations Dashboard Agent aggregates ticket deflection metrics 
Parallel data retrieval from HPO, PE, DataHub, OMC master table — brief assembled in seconds vs. hours manually 
Shared Knowledge Base across supplier agents: RFP config, briefing document, hotel-level instruction set — single source of truth, three agents consume it independently 
Predictive acceptance modeling — agent predicts likelihood a hotel will accept at target rate based on historical patterns, market conditions, and stickiness 
Live negotiation simulation — consultant can "rehearse" against an agent playing the hotel's likely objections 
Expose agents as MCP server for use inside Claude / ChatGPT by consultants in the field 
Cost & savings analytics — track realized savings per agent-assisted negotiation vs. baseline 
Challenge Design Principles: The platform must test both engineering skills (data integration, RAG pipelines, real-time personalization) and effective AI utilization (MCP protocol, agent orchestration, A2A patterns). The traveler intelligence domain provides rich data integration challenges and compelling personalization demos. 
5. Agent Integration: From Platform to Product 
Every intelligence agent you create gets a deployable endpoint. The power is in embedding agents at the exact moment a human needs them — not in a separate tool they must context-switch to. A Negotiation Brief Agent fires the instant Copilot flags a hotel; a Compliance Agent triggers the moment a hotel clicks pre-submit; a Question Resolution Agent responds in the supplier app chat without a single support ticket being raised. One agent, many surfaces — deployed where the user already works. 
